## What do we do?

80,000 Hours provides research and support to help talented people move into careers that tackle [the world’s most pressing problems](https://80000hours.org/problem-profiles/).

After more than 10 years of research over a dozen problem areas, we’re putting most of our focus on helping people work on positively shaping the trajectory of AI because [we think it presents the most serious and urgent challenges the world is facing right now](https://80000hours.org/problem-profiles/artificial-intelligence).

We also think making AGI go well will require addressing a variety of issues and take contributions from a huge number of people with different skills (technical and non-technical). So we think that many people in our audience will be able to help.

What we offer:

1. Our online guides — especially our [guide to having an impactful career](https://80000hours.org/career-guide/), our [profiles on pressing global problems series](https://80000hours.org/problem-profiles/), our [advice on using your career to address risks from advanced AI](https://80000hours.org/agi/), and our [reviews of high-impact career paths](https://80000hours.org/career-reviews)
2. Long-form [documentary-style videos about transformative AI and its risks](https://www.youtube.com/@AI%5FIn%5FContext)
3. Our [podcast](https://80000hours.org/podcast) — in-depth interviews about the world’s most pressing problems and how you can help solve them
4. Our [job board](https://80000hours.org/job-board) — which highlights current opportunities to build skills and work on pressing problems, and provides the opportunity to get matched with particularly promising roles
5. Our [one-on-one service](https://80000hours.org/speak-with-us/?int%5Fcampaign=about-pages) — which provides individualised career advice, helps people make plans and build connections, and matches some advisees to key high-impact roles

We’re a nonprofit funded by philanthropic donations, and you can access all our advice for free. Our career guide was published by Penguin Random House in May 2026\. You can buy the book [here](https://80000hours.org/book/), or read the same material [online](https://80000hours.org/about/80000hours.org/career-guide/).

## How did we get started?

80,000 Hours started in 2011 when our founders, [Ben](https://benjamintodd.org) and [Will](https://www.williammacaskill.com/), were about to graduate from Oxford and were wondering what to do with their own careers.

Like many, they wanted careers that were satisfying, paid the bills, and made a worthwhile contribution to society — but they were really unsure which paths would be best.

The standard advice was to become a teacher, doctor, or charity worker, but these didn’t seem like the only good options. Should they instead go into research? Join a political campaign? Or something else?

It didn’t seem like existing career advice even tried to answer this question, so Ben and Will started doing their own research.

They presented their early ideas in a lecture in February 2011, and to their surprise, several people in the audience decided to totally change their career plans. And several asked them to start an organisation.

Inspired by this feedback, a team was formed and 80,000 Hours was started as a part-time project in 2011.

Our aim from the start has been to provide the advice we wish we’d had: in-depth, based on [the best research available](https://80000hours.org/key-ideas/#what-is-our-advice-based-on), and taking seriously the question of how to do the most good.

Our thinking was that if we could enable just a couple of people to find high-impact careers, that would be like a multiplier on what we could achieve ourselves.

We hired a full-time team in 2012, and since then, we’ve spoken to hundreds of experts, read what relevant literature we could find, and conducted our own analyses of many career paths. Over 3,000 people have told us they’ve changed to higher-impact paths due to our advice.

In early 2025, we [shifted our strategic approach to focus more on helping people work on navigating the transition to a world with powerful AI systems](https://80000hours.org/2025/04/strategic-approach/), which has been our [top-ranked problem](https://80000hours.org/problem-profiles/) since 2016.

We’ll always have more to learn, and [we’ve made some mistakes](https://80000hours.org/about/credibility/evaluations/mistakes/), but we hope that by sharing what we’ve learned, we can help speed up our readers’ journeys toward high-impact careers, and by doing that, help tackle the world’s most pressing problems.

Read more about our progress each year in our [annual reviews](https://80000hours.org/about/credibility/evaluations/). You can also hear Ben tell our founding story in [this podcast](https://80000hours.org/podcast/episodes/ben-todd-key-ideas-of-80000hours/).

## What impact have we had so far?

As of early 2026, we’ve had over 10 million readers on our website, 1.8 million listening hours on our podcast, and have given one-on-one advice to over 7,500 people.

Over 3,000 people have told us that, due to engaging with us, they have significantly changed their career plans and expect to have a larger social impact as a result.

We’ve also been one of the largest drivers of growth in the [effective altruism community](https://80000hours.org/community/).

Some of our readers whose stories we’re especially proud of include:

![Ethan Perez portrait]()

#### Ethan Perez

Ethan Perez was studying computer science when he came across 80,000 Hours, which convinced him that the risk from advanced artificial intelligence was a highly pressing problem. After speaking with an 80,000 Hours advisor, he decided to work full time on AI safety instead of pursuing a career in self-driving car technology. He went on to write his PhD thesis on fixing undesirable behaviour in language models and took a role as a research scientist working on AI safety at Anthropic.  
[Read more](https://80000hours.org/stories/ethan-perez/)

![Katie Hearsum portrait]()

#### Katie Hearsum

Katie was working at a bank, but she had a feeling that she wanted to use her career to help others — she just wasn’t sure exactly how. She spoke to an [80,000 Hours advisor](https://80000hours.org/speak-with-us/?int%5Fcampaign=user-story%5F%5Fkatie-hearsum) who helped her realise she could have an impact by helping to improve the [long-term future](https://80000hours.org/articles/future-generations/) and connected her to a network of people working in this area. After exploring her options, Katie realised she had a strong personal fit for [operations work](https://80000hours.org/articles/operations-management/) and accepted a role in operations at [Longview Philanthropy](https://www.longview.org/), an organisation aimed at reducing the risks of a major global catastrophe. Katie is now the COO of Longview Philanthropy — and a trustee of the 80,000 Hours Foundation.  
[Read more](https://80000hours.org/stories/katie-hearsum/)

![Rashida Polk portrait]()

#### Rashida Polk

Rashida started her career on the front lines of healthcare, working as a nurse in cardiovascular and intensive care units. While she found her work deeply rewarding, she sought a more sustainable way to help even more people over the course of her career. After discovering 80,000 Hours, she realized she could make an even bigger impact by transitioning to [biosecurity policy](https://80000hours.org/career-reviews/biorisk-research/). With inspiration from the 80,000 Hours [career guide](https://80000hours.org/career-guide/) and support from the [1-1 advising team](https://80000hours.org/speak-with-us/), Rashida navigated the challenges of leaving nursing and retooling her healthcare experience for a policy role. She now works as a Congressional Fellow with the Senate Committee on Homeland Security & Governmental Affairs, helping reduce biological risks.  
[Read more](https://80000hours.org/stories/rashida-polk/)

People we’ve worked with have helped to:

* Develop the fields of research around [AI alignment](https://80000hours.org/problem-profiles/risks-from-power-seeking-ai/), [catastrophic pandemics](https://80000hours.org/problem-profiles/preventing-catastrophic-pandemics), and [global priorities](https://80000hours.org/career-reviews/global-priorities-researcher)
* [Build the effective altruism movement](https://www.effectivealtruism.org/articles/introduction-to-effective-altruism)
* Direct tens of millions of dollars to life-saving interventions in the developing world

They include people working in top AI safety organisations and the White House, up-and-coming academics, founders of new nonprofits, and philanthropists.

Hundreds of our readers have also pledged 10% of their income to charity through [Giving What We Can](https://80000hours.org/career-guide/making-a-difference/).

More formally, we evaluate our impact by tracking the number of people who significantly change their career plans because of us. You can read much more about how we evaluate our impact in our [annual reviews](https://80000hours.org/about/credibility/evaluations/).

## Why “80,000 Hours?”

People typically have 80,000 working hours in their careers: 40 years x 50 weeks x 40 hours.

This is a lot of time. If you want to have a positive impact with your life, your career is overwhelmingly likely to be your best opportunity to do it.

People sometimes point out that progress in AI could shorten the average career, perhaps by automating most jobs or even [causing a global catastrophe](https://80000hours.org/problem-profiles/artificial-intelligence/?v=1). We share those concerns. However:

* Even if someone ‘only’ has 40,000 or 20,000 hours left to work, we want to help them use that time well.
* We believe that many careers will remain relevant well past the advent of transformative AI.

Read more about [why we focus on careers and the inspiration behind our name](https://80000hours.org/make-a-difference-with-your-career/).

## What’s our vision and plan for growth?

So many people want to make a difference,[1](#fn-1 "<p>It's hard to find data on the exact proportion of people who want to make a difference, but it's clear that it's a significant priority for a lot of people. For instance, in a <a href="https://80000hours.org/2012/10/net-impact-survey-hopeful-news-for-ethical-careers/">Net Impact survey</a>, 31% of respondents reported that making a difference was 'essential' in their choice of career and 45% reported that they would take a 15% pay cut to make more of a difference. In a <a href="http://www.webcitation.org/6TNAV7CSg">Guardian survey</a>, over 70% of respondents reported that ethical considerations were 'crucial' in choosing an employer. In a <a href="http://www.bentley.edu/centers/center-for-women-and-business/millennials-workplace">Bentley University survey</a>, Multigenerational Impacts on the Workplace, 84% reported that "knowing I am helping to make a positive difference in the world is more important to me than professional recognition." A <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/sustainable-business/2015/jan/15/employers-social-environmental-business-practices-survey">survey of millennials by Global Tolerance</a> found that "42% of individuals...want to work for an organisation that has a positive impact on the world." Meaningful work that benefited others was more important than a high salary for 44% of the respondents. At the same time, people are often dissatisfied with existing advice. For instance, in a 2012 <a href="https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm201213/cmselect/cmeduc/632/632vw66.htm">British Youth Council survey</a>, 80% of 12–26 year olds reported that formal careers advice was 'a little bit' or 'not at all' helpful.</p>
") but most career advice doesn’t even discuss the question of which career paths have the most impact.

Our aim is to become the best source of advice and support for talented people who prioritise impact, helping them find satisfying careers that fulfil their potential to contribute to the world.

By doing this, we hope to inspire the next generation of leaders tackling the world’s [most pressing problems](https://80000hours.org/problem-profiles/).

Right now, we’re especially focused on helping people work on making advanced AI go well, because we think [this is probably the best way to achieve our high-level, cause-impartial goal of helping people have high-impact careers](https://80000hours.org/2025/04/strategic-approach/).

We’d also love to spark a global conversation about how people can best use their careers to help the world, and to inspire more people to take a high-impact path. We want the new default to be that everyone with the privilege to have options in their career asks themselves: “Which global problems are most pressing? And how can I best use my career to tackle them?”

You can see much more detail about our progress and our plans for the coming years in our [annual reviews](https://80000hours.org/about/credibility/evaluations/).

## How are we funded?

We’re a nonprofit funded primarily by [Coefficient Giving](https://coefficientgiving.org/). You can donate to us [here](https://80000hours.org/support-us/donate/).

Due to charity law, there are some restrictions on our activities. For example, we cannot recommend people work with or donate to particular political parties or electoral candidates.

We don’t accept any advertising money or corporate sponsorships.

## Who is our advice aimed at?

Our aim is to do the most we can to help solve big neglected problems. Given our limited capacity, we need to prioritise which audiences we work with as well as which career paths and issues we cover, in order to make the biggest positive difference we can.

Our advice is focused on people who have options for how to spend their careers and the security and ability to make helping the world one of their main goals.

We especially focus on people 18–45 living in English-speaking countries, especially the US or UK, who want to take an analytical approach to doing good, in part because this is the career landscape we know best.

Some of our resources, such as our list of [high-impact career paths](https://80000hours.org/career-reviews/#our-priority-paths) and [one-on-one advice](https://80000hours.org/speak-with-us/?int%5Fcampaign=about-pages) — where we have especially limited capacity — are aimed at people who are unusually ambitious and high-performing, because this allows us to achieve a lot even if we only work with a small number of people.

The core principles we cover, such as the advice in our [career guide](https://80000hours.org/career-guide/), our [list of top skills to build](https://80000hours.org/skills/), and our [advice on career planning](https://80000hours.org/career-planning/summary/), apply to anyone who wants to make impact a priority.

If you’re not able to change your career right now, see our [advice on how to have a big impact without changing your job](https://80000hours.org/career-guide/making-a-difference/), through advocacy, political engagement, and donations.

Many people are not in a position to spend time on social impact, let alone make it a large focus, and that’s fine — we also have many priorities in life besides impact. But if you are in such a position, we hope we can help.

Learn more in [advice on how to read our advice](https://80000hours.org/articles/advice-on-how-to-read-our-advice/).

## Who is on the team?

We have a full-time team of more than 50 people. You can read about them and some of our research advisors on our [team page](https://80000hours.org/about/meet-the-team/).

## Which partners do we work with?

* We consider ourselves a [part of the effective altruism community](https://forum.effectivealtruism.org/posts/iCDcJdqqmBa9QrEHv/faq-on-the-relationship-between-80-000-hours-and-the) and often draw on the work of others committed to using evidence and reason to find the best ways to help others. For instance, we draw on the work of [Coefficient Giving](https://www.openphilanthropy.org/), a philanthropic and research foundation that is 80,000 Hours’ biggest funder.
* We often work with researchers at the University of Oxford, including several of our past board members.
* We also consider ourselves part of the AI safety community, and often seek input from leading groups in the area, such as [Forethought](https://www.forethought.org/).
* Until April 1, 2025, 80,000 Hours was a project of the [Effective Ventures group](https://www.ev.org/) — the umbrella term for Effective Ventures Foundation and Effective Ventures Foundation USA, Inc. — which are two separate legal entities that work together. Other projects under this umbrella include [Giving What We Can](https://www.givingwhatwecan.org/) and the [Centre for Effective Altruism](https://www.centreforeffectivealtruism.org/).
* In 2015, [we attended Y Combinator](https://80000hours.org/2015/08/why-is-80000-hours-in-y-combinator-as-a-non-profit-and-whats-it-like/), one of the world’s top startup accelerators.
* See a list of [some of our external advisors and board members](https://80000hours.org/about/meet-the-team/).

## Our cultural values

We aim to uphold the following values in carrying out our work:

1. **Ambitious long-term impact** — will a given action or approach make us into the world’s best source of career advice for people who want to maximise their positive impact?
2. **A modest, scientific mindset** — why might our views be wrong?
3. **Openness and honesty** — default to [transparency](https://80000hours.org/about/credibility/evaluations/mistakes/) and take the ‘red face test.’

We are also currently focused on fostering a fast, innovative, AI-focused internal culture.

### Diversity and inclusion

We strive to be a workplace where people from different backgrounds thrive. This includes people from different races, gender identities, sexual orientations, nationalities, and beyond. We recognise that because of historical and continuing oppression and inequality, this requires sustained effort. We also endorse [CEA’s stance on diversity and inclusion](https://www.centreforeffectivealtruism.org/diversity-and-inclusion) in the effective altruism community and within organisations.

## How can you get involved?

* To learn about our high-impact career advice, read our [career guide](https://80000hours.org/career-guide/)
* To learn more about why and how to help make advanced AI go well, check out our [AI hub](https://80000hours.org/ai/)
* To go in-depth with experts on the world’s most pressing problems, listen to [our podcast](https://80000hours.org/podcast/)
* To stay up to date on new research and jobs, [join our newsletter](https://80000hours.org/newsletter/)
* Find out how to meet people in our community
* For journalistic requests, including for interviews and background information, email press@80000hours.org
* To subscribe to quarterly updates on our progress as an organisation, please email [info@80000hours.org](mailto:info@80000hours.org)

### Notes and references

1. It’s hard to find data on the exact proportion of people who want to make a difference, but it’s clear that it’s a significant priority for a lot of people. For instance, in a [Net Impact survey](https://80000hours.org/2012/10/net-impact-survey-hopeful-news-for-ethical-careers/), 31% of respondents reported that making a difference was ‘essential’ in their choice of career and 45% reported that they would take a 15% pay cut to make more of a difference. In a [Guardian survey](http://www.webcitation.org/6TNAV7CSg), over 70% of respondents reported that ethical considerations were ‘crucial’ in choosing an employer. In a [Bentley University survey](http://www.bentley.edu/centers/center-for-women-and-business/millennials-workplace), Multigenerational Impacts on the Workplace, 84% reported that “knowing I am helping to make a positive difference in the world is more important to me than professional recognition.” A [survey of millennials by Global Tolerance](https://www.theguardian.com/sustainable-business/2015/jan/15/employers-social-environmental-business-practices-survey) found that “42% of individuals…want to work for an organisation that has a positive impact on the world.” Meaningful work that benefited others was more important than a high salary for 44% of the respondents. At the same time, people are often dissatisfied with existing advice. For instance, in a 2012 [British Youth Council survey](https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm201213/cmselect/cmeduc/632/632vw66.htm), 80% of 12–26 year olds reported that formal careers advice was ‘a little bit’ or ‘not at all’ helpful.[↩](#fn-ref-1)

